WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:04.720
All right, friends.

00:04.720 --> 00:08.400
So now we're going to go to the process of creating partitions in SQL.

00:08.440 --> 00:12.520
At the start it might sound a little bit complicated, but we're going to do it step by step.

00:12.520 --> 00:14.160
And I have a sketch for that.

00:14.160 --> 00:18.720
So we have like four steps because we have in the database like multiple layers.

00:18.720 --> 00:20.320
So let's see how we can do that.

00:20.320 --> 00:21.120
Let's go.

00:21.120 --> 00:24.880
So the first step is that we're going to go and define the partition function.

00:24.880 --> 00:30.840
So what it does we're going to go and define here in the function the logic on how to divide the table

00:30.840 --> 00:32.000
into partitions.

00:32.000 --> 00:34.480
And this can be based on the partition key.

00:34.640 --> 00:37.800
So that means we need a column in order to define the logic.

00:37.800 --> 00:42.360
And we usually use columns with the dates like for example the order dates.

00:42.360 --> 00:46.120
Or in other scenarios we can use the region or country and so on.

00:46.120 --> 00:48.160
But the most famous one is the dates.

00:48.160 --> 00:51.920
And that's because our tables like get bigger over the time.

00:51.920 --> 00:54.160
And there are like multiple types of functions.

00:54.160 --> 00:56.440
We're going to focus on the range function.

00:56.440 --> 00:59.600
So how it's going to work we're going to have like a range of dates.

00:59.600 --> 01:02.910
And then we have to define like boundary values.

01:02.950 --> 01:06.470
And let's say that I would like to make a partition for each year.

01:06.630 --> 01:10.510
And in order to do that, we have to define the partition boundary.

01:10.510 --> 01:12.030
So it is like a value.

01:12.070 --> 01:16.550
The boundary of the years could be like the first day of the year or the last day of the year.

01:16.630 --> 01:21.030
So here in this example we're going to take for the boundary the last day of the year.

01:21.030 --> 01:25.230
So the last day of 2023, 2024 and 2025.

01:25.270 --> 01:29.150
So we call those values the boundary of our function.

01:29.150 --> 01:32.230
Now between the boundaries we're going to have our partitions.

01:32.310 --> 01:38.990
So for example all the rows for 2025 and earlier years it's going to be the partition one.

01:38.990 --> 01:42.470
So between the boundary and everything before is one partition.

01:42.470 --> 01:45.590
And after that between the two boundaries we have partition two.

01:45.630 --> 01:49.510
So this partition is going to be for all rows of 2024.

01:49.550 --> 01:54.990
And then we have another section the partition three where we have all rows of 2025.

01:55.030 --> 01:59.950
And then between the last boundary and everything onwards it's going to be partition four.

01:59.990 --> 02:03.500
And here we're going to have all the rows from 2026 26 onwards.

02:03.500 --> 02:05.180
So that we have now a logic.

02:05.220 --> 02:10.140
We are telling SQL how to divide our data into multiple partitions.

02:10.140 --> 02:13.380
And here there is like two methods the left and the right.

02:13.380 --> 02:15.180
So what are those two methods.

02:15.180 --> 02:16.940
So again we have our boundary.

02:16.940 --> 02:21.660
And now the big question to which partition does this boundary belongs to.

02:21.700 --> 02:23.860
Is it partition one or partition two.

02:23.900 --> 02:25.780
And that's why we have those two methods.

02:25.780 --> 02:30.260
If you say it is left that means the boundary belongs to the partition number one.

02:30.260 --> 02:35.540
But in the other hand, if you say it is right, then the boundary is going to be part and belongs to

02:35.580 --> 02:36.940
the partition number two.

02:36.980 --> 02:41.820
So you have to decide whether the boundaries belongs to the left partition or to the right partition.

02:41.820 --> 02:47.660
And with that, in the partition one, we are going to have all the rows of 2023, including the last

02:47.660 --> 02:52.860
day of 2023, because in the partition two, we only focus on 2024.

02:52.980 --> 02:56.260
So it's just the boundary belongs to the left partition.

02:56.260 --> 02:57.100
It's very simple.

02:57.140 --> 02:59.340
Now let's go and implement that in SQL.

02:59.340 --> 03:00.300
So let's do it.

03:00.300 --> 03:01.900
The syntax is very simple.

03:01.900 --> 03:05.860
We're going to say create Its partition function.

03:06.100 --> 03:07.580
And then we have to give it the name.

03:07.580 --> 03:13.060
So it's going to be partition by year since we are dividing the data by the year.

03:13.260 --> 03:16.420
And after that we have to define the data type.

03:16.420 --> 03:19.020
So we are splitting the data by a date.

03:19.020 --> 03:20.260
So it's going to be date.

03:20.300 --> 03:23.860
And after that we have to define the partition function type.

03:24.020 --> 03:26.700
So in our example we are using the range.

03:26.700 --> 03:29.900
And now we have to define whether it is left or right.

03:30.100 --> 03:31.620
We're going to stick with the left.

03:32.100 --> 03:34.100
And now comes the very important step.

03:34.100 --> 03:36.340
We have to define the boundaries.

03:36.340 --> 03:38.580
So we're going to say for values.

03:39.460 --> 03:41.580
And we're going to enter here three boundaries.

03:41.580 --> 03:45.100
Like in our example for each year we're going to define a date.

03:45.100 --> 03:49.180
So 2023 and the last day of the year.

03:49.380 --> 03:51.540
Same goes for 2024.

03:52.580 --> 03:55.780
And for the last one 2025.

03:57.140 --> 04:00.180
So with that we have defined the logic the range.

04:00.180 --> 04:02.180
We have defined the boundaries.

04:02.180 --> 04:04.900
And we tell the SQL the boundaries are a date.

04:04.900 --> 04:04.930
idiots.

04:04.930 --> 04:08.130
So let's go and execute our function.

04:08.170 --> 04:09.050
Okay, so that's it.

04:09.050 --> 04:10.370
As you can see, it's very simple.

04:10.370 --> 04:15.690
We just created a function that split the data by the date using the range left.

04:15.690 --> 04:20.170
And of course this function is not yet attached to any tables or anything.

04:20.210 --> 04:23.130
It is just a logic that is stored in the database.

04:23.170 --> 04:23.530
All right.

04:23.530 --> 04:28.930
So now since our partition function is stored inside the database, we will have metadata about those

04:28.930 --> 04:31.770
functions stored in the system schema.

04:31.770 --> 04:34.930
So we have there a dedicated table called partition functions.

04:34.930 --> 04:39.930
And there we can find informations about all functions that we have inside our database.

04:39.930 --> 04:41.290
So let's go and execute it.

04:41.290 --> 04:45.210
And as you can see we find now our new created partition function.

04:45.210 --> 04:49.530
So partition by year it is a range and it has an ID and so on.

04:49.530 --> 04:53.810
And I really recommend you to check it before creating any new partition function.

04:53.810 --> 04:56.090
Maybe you have already one in the projects.

05:00.090 --> 05:00.370
Okay.

05:00.370 --> 05:02.850
So now let's check the next step in our process.

05:02.850 --> 05:05.810
We're going to go and build now the file groups.

05:05.840 --> 05:07.440
So what is a file group?

05:07.480 --> 05:10.880
It is like a logical container of one or more.

05:11.040 --> 05:12.120
Data files.

05:12.120 --> 05:13.040
So it's very simple.

05:13.040 --> 05:15.440
It's like folders we're going to go and create now.

05:15.480 --> 05:16.960
Like multiple folders.

05:16.960 --> 05:20.320
So later we can insert inside them files.

05:20.320 --> 05:25.640
And this is really nice because it gives us like freedom and flexibility where we can go and decide

05:25.640 --> 05:29.120
how the data files are organized for each partition.

05:29.120 --> 05:33.320
So what we usually do, we go and create for each partition a file group.

05:33.320 --> 05:39.000
So we're going to have like four folders or four file groups for 2023, 2024 and so on.

05:39.000 --> 05:41.360
So now let's go back to SQL in order to do that.

05:41.400 --> 05:41.720
All right.

05:41.720 --> 05:43.840
So now let's go and create those file groups.

05:43.840 --> 05:45.080
The syntax is very simple.

05:45.080 --> 05:47.920
So it's going to say alter database.

05:47.920 --> 05:52.600
And now we have to tell the database where these file groups should be stored in which database.

05:52.600 --> 05:54.840
So I'm going to stay with that sales DB.

05:55.080 --> 05:58.400
And then we have to tell okay adds file group.

05:58.680 --> 06:01.480
And after that we have to define the name of the file group.

06:01.480 --> 06:04.160
So the first one is going to be for 2023.

06:04.160 --> 06:06.040
So the syntax is very simple.

06:06.080 --> 06:08.910
Let's go and do it for the other years.

06:08.910 --> 06:12.870
So we need 20, 24, five and six.

06:12.910 --> 06:13.270
Okay.

06:13.310 --> 06:14.070
So that's all.

06:14.070 --> 06:16.470
We can just select everything and execute.

06:16.590 --> 06:18.150
So as you can see it's very simple.

06:18.150 --> 06:20.630
We have just created four file groups.

06:20.790 --> 06:21.670
And they are empty.

06:21.670 --> 06:24.310
So we don't have anything inside those containers.

06:24.350 --> 06:28.630
Now let's say that you have made a mistake with the Namings and so on, and you would like to drop one

06:28.630 --> 06:29.150
of them.

06:29.350 --> 06:31.710
So the syntax is as well very easy.

06:32.110 --> 06:34.470
So it's going to say alter database sales db.

06:34.510 --> 06:37.190
And instead of ads you're going to say remove.

06:37.390 --> 06:41.310
So once you execute this this file group will be dropped.

06:41.310 --> 06:42.590
But we need it.

06:42.590 --> 06:44.670
So let's go and recreate it.

06:44.710 --> 06:49.990
Now as usual after creating stuff, let's check whether everything is created correctly and whether

06:49.990 --> 06:52.430
we have any duplicate or anything wrong.

06:52.590 --> 06:58.830
So with that, we have as well a file group table inside the system schema and let's go and execute

06:58.830 --> 06:58.950
it.

06:58.950 --> 07:02.430
So I'm just filtering with the type FG for file group.

07:02.430 --> 07:03.670
So let's execute it.

07:03.670 --> 07:07.630
And now we can see in our database we have four file groups.

07:07.670 --> 07:10.750
Now four of those five groups we just created, right.

07:10.750 --> 07:13.750
So we have that 20, 23, 24 and so on.

07:13.750 --> 07:16.670
But we have something called primary File group.

07:16.710 --> 07:21.150
This is the default file group that is created for each database.

07:21.270 --> 07:24.470
So it is a container for all data files in your database.

07:24.470 --> 07:27.750
And as you can see we have here a flag saying it is a default.

07:27.750 --> 07:28.470
So it's default.

07:28.470 --> 07:29.310
And we have it one.

07:29.310 --> 07:31.470
And for the rest there are another defaults.

07:31.470 --> 07:36.310
So this is really nice to see all the file groups inside your database to check that you don't have

07:36.350 --> 07:37.630
duplicate and so on.

07:41.670 --> 07:42.110
Okay.

07:42.150 --> 07:45.270
Now moving on to the third step where things can get more physically.

07:45.270 --> 07:49.550
So so far we have like a function the file group and all those stuff are logical stuff.

07:49.550 --> 07:50.830
We don't have data yet.

07:50.870 --> 07:54.670
In order to have data we have to go and create data files.

07:54.670 --> 07:58.350
So as we learned before, data files can contain our actual data.

07:58.350 --> 08:00.910
And they're going to be stored physically in the database.

08:00.910 --> 08:06.030
So you can go and assign for each file group like one or multiple data files.

08:06.030 --> 08:13.020
And the file format here is it is secondary data files we have like primary and secondary, but in the

08:13.020 --> 08:15.660
partitions we usually go with this format the MDF.

08:15.780 --> 08:21.660
So again the file groups are a logical containers and the data files are physical files where our actual

08:21.660 --> 08:23.380
data can be stored inside it.

08:23.420 --> 08:26.740
So now let's go back to SQL in order to create some data files.

08:26.780 --> 08:26.980
Okay.

08:27.020 --> 08:31.100
So now we're going to come to a little bit annoying part where we're going to go and create files.

08:31.100 --> 08:33.020
But the syntax is as well very simple.

08:33.020 --> 08:35.940
So it's going to say the same things alter database.

08:36.100 --> 08:38.100
And our database is sales DB.

08:38.140 --> 08:40.900
And then this time I'm going to say add file.

08:41.140 --> 08:46.100
And now we have to give SQL not only the name but the physical place of the files.

08:46.100 --> 08:47.340
So let's do it step by step.

08:47.340 --> 08:49.620
We're going to open U2 parentheses.

08:49.620 --> 08:53.020
So first we have to define for SQL the logical name.

08:53.020 --> 08:54.260
It is not the file name.

08:54.260 --> 08:56.140
It is the logical name of the file.

08:56.140 --> 09:00.500
So let's give it a name for example B 2023 and then comma.

09:00.500 --> 09:03.300
So this is the logical name.

09:03.340 --> 09:08.260
And now the next one is we're going to give the physical name of the file together with the path.

09:08.260 --> 09:11.090
So we're going to say file name equal.

09:11.130 --> 09:15.530
And now we have to define for SQL the complete path of the file.

09:15.530 --> 09:19.610
In SQL server there is like a default path where the data can be stored.

09:19.730 --> 09:22.050
And I'm going to go and use the same path.

09:22.050 --> 09:24.330
And the path really depends on the version.

09:24.330 --> 09:27.410
And as will the type of the SQL server that you are using.

09:27.410 --> 09:32.850
So for the current version that I'm using for this tutorial, we can find it over here in this path.

09:32.850 --> 09:37.930
So if we go to the C then program files Microsoft SQL server MySQL.

09:38.130 --> 09:43.810
And the version for me is 16 SQL express and then inside MySQL data and so on.

09:43.810 --> 09:46.010
So we're going to go inside this folder.

09:46.010 --> 09:49.370
And now we can see over here all the database files.

09:49.370 --> 09:53.170
So we can see for example here the sales DB the sales DB logs.

09:53.210 --> 09:55.290
And we have here the Adventureworks and so on.

09:55.290 --> 09:58.290
So you're going to see all the files of your database and what we're going to do.

09:58.290 --> 10:02.730
We're going to put as well our partitions files inside the default folder.

10:02.730 --> 10:07.730
But for real projects you have to ask the database administrators about the exact location where you

10:07.730 --> 10:09.530
can put your partitions.

10:09.530 --> 10:11.160
So let's go back to SQL.

10:11.160 --> 10:13.560
And I'm going to put this path over here.

10:13.560 --> 10:16.360
And then we have to specify the file name.

10:16.360 --> 10:19.360
So it's going to be P 2023 dots.

10:19.400 --> 10:21.280
And now we have to specify the file name.

10:21.280 --> 10:22.280
So in DF.

10:22.480 --> 10:25.640
And with that we have now a complete path with the file name.

10:25.640 --> 10:28.160
So we are almost there but we are not done yet.

10:28.200 --> 10:33.320
We have to tell SQL where to put this file in which container in which file group.

10:33.320 --> 10:37.520
So we're going to go over here and we're going to say two file group.

10:38.000 --> 10:40.200
And here make sure to select the correct one.

10:40.200 --> 10:42.960
So FG 2023.

10:43.000 --> 10:43.520
All right.

10:43.520 --> 10:46.120
So that's all let's go and execute it.

10:46.160 --> 10:47.720
So let's do it.

10:47.720 --> 10:53.000
And with that we have created a file inside a file group I will not be creating like multiple files

10:53.000 --> 10:54.120
inside one file group.

10:54.120 --> 10:55.520
It's going to be like 1 to 1.

10:55.520 --> 10:59.840
So now what we're going to do we're going to go and create the other files for each file group for each

10:59.880 --> 11:00.240
year.

11:00.360 --> 11:03.640
So we just have to copy and paste and just change the names.

11:04.000 --> 11:07.680
So for 2024 it's going to be like this.

11:08.920 --> 11:10.160
So that's it.

11:10.200 --> 11:13.840
And the same thing for 2025.

11:17.200 --> 11:19.960
And for the last one, 2026.

11:24.960 --> 11:29.000
And we can go and select our everything and execute it.

11:29.000 --> 11:29.800
So that's it.

11:29.840 --> 11:33.120
With that we have created now four different files.

11:33.120 --> 11:37.120
And we have mapped as well each file to the correct file group.

11:37.120 --> 11:42.720
And I usually don't create like a lot of files I just create like one for each year or maybe for bunch

11:42.720 --> 11:43.240
of years.

11:43.240 --> 11:47.680
So you don't have to go and make for each day, like a partition or something like that.

11:47.720 --> 11:51.440
Okay, as usual, after creating stuff, we have to go and check the metadata.

11:51.480 --> 11:57.520
Now I have prepared a query where we query the file groups together with the files, so all the data

11:57.520 --> 12:00.560
informations could be found inside the table master files.

12:00.560 --> 12:03.680
And then we join those tables and select our database.

12:03.680 --> 12:05.760
So let's go and query this one.

12:05.760 --> 12:09.920
And now we're going to get a list of all files inside your database.

12:09.920 --> 12:14.510
So we see over here we have the primary for the database itself.

12:14.510 --> 12:17.950
And you can see the path of the file and as well the size of it.

12:17.950 --> 12:20.430
And we can see over here we have four files.

12:20.430 --> 12:25.270
And the file group that is assigned to and the complete path of each file.

12:25.270 --> 12:30.710
And you can monitor over here of course how the size of each file is growing over the time.

12:30.750 --> 12:32.910
Maybe one of them is getting like really big.

12:32.910 --> 12:36.670
And then you can think about let's go and split it to multiple files.

12:36.670 --> 12:39.270
So that's it about how to create data files.

12:43.310 --> 12:43.630
All right.

12:43.630 --> 12:48.030
So now we're going to move to the last step where we're going to go and define the function scheme.

12:48.030 --> 12:52.310
Now if you have a look to this picture you see that there is something missing from one side.

12:52.310 --> 12:56.390
We have defined how to divide our data into multiple partitions.

12:56.390 --> 13:00.830
And from the other side we have prepared all the files and the file groups and so on.

13:00.830 --> 13:06.950
And now what is missing is the connection how to connect those partitions to the file groups.

13:06.950 --> 13:09.830
And we can do that by using the partition scheme.

13:09.830 --> 13:15.500
So all what we are doing now is just defining which partition belongs to which file group.

13:15.500 --> 13:20.300
So for example we're going to go unmap the partition one to the file group 2023.

13:20.300 --> 13:23.580
And with that all the data of 2023 and earlier.

13:23.780 --> 13:26.620
Gonna go to the file group 2023.

13:26.660 --> 13:30.300
And of course we have to go and map each partition to a file group.

13:30.300 --> 13:33.020
If you don't do that you will get error in SQL.

13:33.060 --> 13:39.540
And once we build the partition scheme then we can have all the components ready in order to have partition

13:39.540 --> 13:40.220
table.

13:40.220 --> 13:42.060
So now let's have a quick summarize.

13:42.060 --> 13:47.300
The partition function can decide on how to split your data into multiple partitions.

13:47.500 --> 13:53.980
The partition scheme can go and map the partitions to a file group, and the file groups are like folders

13:53.980 --> 14:00.180
in order to organize your files, and each file group has one or more data files where your actual data

14:00.220 --> 14:03.140
are going to be stored physically at these files.

14:03.180 --> 14:08.180
At the start, it might be confusing, but now as you understand each layer, then it can make it easier

14:08.180 --> 14:09.980
for you to build partitions.

14:09.980 --> 14:14.100
So now let's go back to SQL in order to build the partition scheme okay.

14:14.140 --> 14:17.570
So now we have the easiest part where we're going to connect everything together.

14:17.570 --> 14:19.170
So the syntax is very simple.

14:19.170 --> 14:22.570
It's going to say create partition scheme.

14:22.930 --> 14:24.730
And now we have to give it a name.

14:24.730 --> 14:29.650
So let's go with like scheme partition by ear.

14:29.850 --> 14:33.450
And now we have to map the partition function with the file groups.

14:33.450 --> 14:40.130
So first we're going to say as and then we define here the partition function so as partition.

14:40.610 --> 14:46.130
And now we need the partition function that we have created to as partition by year.

14:46.530 --> 14:49.810
And then after that we're going to map it to the file groups.

14:49.810 --> 14:53.730
And here it is very important to map it in the correct order.

14:53.730 --> 14:55.770
So the order is very important.

14:55.770 --> 14:59.090
So the first one was file group 2023.

14:59.370 --> 15:01.650
The second one 2024.

15:01.850 --> 15:07.490
And we have 2025 and the last one 2026.

15:07.490 --> 15:10.010
So again the order is very important.

15:10.010 --> 15:12.090
And as well it's going to be a little bit tricky.

15:12.090 --> 15:16.730
So sometimes as you are creating like the functions, maybe you make a mistake that you don't know how

15:16.730 --> 15:18.890
much partitions SQL can create.

15:18.930 --> 15:23.330
Like in our example, we have three boundaries and SQL can create four partitions.

15:23.330 --> 15:26.450
So it happens sometimes that you think okay I have three boundaries.

15:26.450 --> 15:31.050
And then I'm going to get three partitions which is not really correct.

15:31.050 --> 15:33.650
So for example let me just remove one of those.

15:34.170 --> 15:36.770
And let's say I have only three file groups.

15:36.810 --> 15:39.370
And let's go and execute this one over here.

15:39.410 --> 15:40.490
Now we are getting error.

15:40.530 --> 15:45.810
It says the partition function generates more partitions than the file groups.

15:45.810 --> 15:51.330
And that is really correct because our definition of the logic can split the data into four partitions.

15:51.330 --> 15:55.610
And now we are giving SQL only three file groups which is not correct.

15:55.610 --> 15:58.610
So we have to go and add the last one.

15:58.610 --> 15:59.170
And one more thing.

15:59.210 --> 16:04.210
SQL will not go and check whether you are mapping things correctly to the file groups, because it doesn't

16:04.210 --> 16:06.810
really care about the naming of those file groups.

16:06.810 --> 16:10.570
So for example, if you go and put this one at the end, what's going to happen?

16:10.570 --> 16:12.210
It's going to be a big problem.

16:12.210 --> 16:19.000
So all the years of 2023 are going to be stored inside 2024, Over 2024 and 2025.

16:19.120 --> 16:22.440
